 "exploratory_or_post_hoc": [
  "P1's restriction to k ≥ 10 was chosen after seeing S1.1 (2026-10-10), before any E0 score (plan §7.1 disclosure).",
  "The e0-review exploratory file, labelled EXPLORATORY and post hoc, holds three analyses. (a) P1 on m ≥ 2048 only: median sum 0.0046, with the k = 100 cells at ≥ 2.25× chance. (b) The k = 1 comparison with S1.1: median sum on k ∈ {1, 10} 0.091 against 0.055, and over all k 0.058 against 0.030. (c) A per-ViT P2b decomposition for arms P and A. The new-pair sums themselves are pre-specified (plan §7.1); the comparison with S1.1 and its reading (k = 1 is where Theorem 1's square-root term is relatively largest) are post hoc.",
  "Some readings of descriptive numbers were made after the labels were known. The new slopes sit about halfway between the level line and the ViT values (λ̂ about 0.44). Caption averaging flattens the slope faster than the level alone predicts (median γ 0.435 against 0.228). Arm A's λ̂ of 0.957 averages ViT-level deviations of opposite sign.",
  "The level gap to Schnaus et al. is roughly fivefold. Attributing it to the vision features (mid-depth CLS here, pooled output tokens there) is a hypothesis; X3 would test it and has not run. Schnaus et al.'s values are readings of their figures.",
  "Paper wording. The e0-review proposals A–F (OLD → NEW; v4 unedited) use wording that plan §13 does not fix, except where they fill §13.4. They are post hoc and, if used, are labelled so in the manuscript source comments (plan B8.3). Proposal D departs from the §13.4 Limitations draft, because that draft reads as if the encoders explained the gap; proposal E replaces §13.5.",
  "The caption-source fact was checked after the results (by the review, and rechecked here). It is a fact about the data, not a statistic, and it changes no result."
 ],
 "not_run": {
